% n23_occurs_cycle -- THE UNBOUNDED RECURSION, reproduced rather than reviewed.
%
% ROADMAP F7 said `k_conv`, `k_shift`, `k_subst` and `k_occurs` "recurse with NO
% fuel bound, so a crafted deep term can still crash the kernel".  DEPTH is not the
% reachable route (a .core file caps at 1024 nodes, so a term caps at ~512 deep and
% 2000-deep recursion was measured NOT to overflow the stack).  The reachable route
% is a CYCLE, and only k_occurs had one, because it alone selected its structural
% tags by a RANGE -- `tg >= 4` -- which silently enrolled tag 7 `num`.
%
% Node 1 is a NUMERAL whose value is 4.  A numeral's k_a is its VALUE, but k_occurs
% read it as a node id, and 4 is the uid of node 3 -- the `pi` whose own domain is
% node 1.  k_occurs(num) -> k_occurs(pi) -> k_occurs(num) -> ... forever.
% Reached through positivity: k_check_inductive_ctor -> k_arg_pos -> k_occurs.
%
% BEFORE the fix: `./seed/build/seed ./tkernel.bin bench/kernel_neg/n23_occurs_cycle.core`
%   -> `trap 82: SIGSEGV/SIGBUS (stack overflow or wild access)`, rc=82.
% AFTER:  rejected 22 -- a numeral is a VALUE, so a `pi` cannot take one as its
% domain.  The twin rejects it for the same rule, reached without the cycle: its
% terms are trees, so the forged edge is unrepresentable there at all.
0 sort 0
axiom I64 : 0
numtype I64
1 num 4
2 const Bad
3 pi 1 2
4 const Bad
5 pi 3 4
6 sort 0
inductive Bad : 6 | mk : 5
